  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=-1,\\,{f}^{\u2032}(2)=3,\\,g(2)=2,\\,{g}^{\u2032}(2)=-3\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=2.$ \n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x)) & =f(x){g}^{\u2032}(x)+g(x){f}^{\u2032}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2} & =f(2){g}^{\u2032}(2)+g(2){f}^{\u2032}(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
